otlib_support(method_name)`\n    - `line 1291`: `prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(`\n- artifact: `sklearn/linear_model/_logistic.py:LogisticRegression.predict_proba`\n  - module: `sklearn.linear_model._logistic`\n  - definition: `lines 1637-1679`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1663`\n  - observed lines: `1663, 1665, 1666, 1667, 1669, 1670`\n  - trace activity: 9 events, 1 calls, 7 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1663`: `check_is_fitted(self)`\n    - `line 1665`: `ovr = self.multi_class in [\"ovr\", \"warn\"] or (`\n    - `line 1666`: `self.multi_class == \"auto\"`\n- artifact: `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py:_check_classifier_response_method`\n  - module: `sklearn.metrics._plot.base`\n  - definition: `lines 6-49`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 26`\n  - observed lines: `26, 31, 32, 33, 34, 49`\n  - trace activity: 8 events, 1 calls, 6 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 26`: `if response_method not in (\"predict_proba\", \"decision_function\", \"auto\"):`\n    - `line 31`: `error_msg = \"response method {} is not defined in {}\"`\n    - `line 32`: `if response_method != \"auto\":`\n- artifact: `sklearn/base.py:is_classifier`\n  - module: `sklearn.base`\n  - definition: `lines 922-935`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 935`\n  - observed lines: `935`\n  - trace activity: 6 events, 2 calls, 2 line events, 2 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 935`: `return getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None) == \"classifier\"`\n- artifact: `sklearn/calibration.py:CalibrationDisplay.__init__`\n  - module: `sklearn.calibration`\n  - definition: `lines 1018-1022`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1019`\n  - observed lines: `1019, 1020, 1021, 1022`\n  - trace activity: 6 events, 1 calls, 4 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1019`: `self.prob_true = prob_true`\n    - `line 1020`: `self.prob_pred = prob_pred`\n    - `line 1021`: `self.y_prob = y_prob`\n- artifact: `sklearn/linear_model/_base.py:LinearClassifierMixin._predict_proba_lr`\n  - module: `sklearn.linear_model._base`\n  - definition: `lines 432-446`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 439`\n  - observed lines: `439, 440, 441, 442`\n  - trace activity: 6 events, 1 calls, 4 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 4\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 439`: `prob = self.decision_function(X)`\n    - `line 440`: `expit(prob, out=prob)`\n    - `line 441`: `if prob.ndim == 1:`\n- artifact: `sklearn/linear_model/_base.py:LinearClassifierMixin.decision_function`\n  - module: `sklearn.linear_model._base`\n  - definition: `lines 386-409`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 405`\n  - observed lines: `405, 407, 408, 409`\n  - trace activity: 6 events, 1 calls, 4 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 5\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 405`: `check_is_fitted(self)`\n    - `line 407`: `X = self._validate_data(X, accept_sparse=\"csr\", reset=False)`\n    - `line 408`: `scores = safe_sparse_dot(X, self.coef_.T, dense_output=True) + self.intercept_`"}, {"content": "I'll help analyze this feature request systematically using the available tools.\n\nLet me start by exploring the repository structure and relevant files.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['calibration_curve', 'plot_calibration', 'CalibrationDisplay']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_get_feature_names\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_digits\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_diabetes\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_diabetes\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_linnerud\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_digits\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_boston\nsklearn/datasets/_california_housing.py:fetch_california_housing\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_sample_image\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_linnerud\n##Searching for term \"calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `calibration_curve`.\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot_calibration\", \"CalibrationDisplay\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot_calibration`.\nJust show the structure of this file due to response length limitations:\n```\n\n\nn_samples = 50000\nn_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n\n# Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n# half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n# blob is therefore 0.5.\ncenters = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\nX, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n                  random_state=42)\n\ny[:n_samples // 2] = 0\ny[n_samples // 2:] = 1\nsample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n\n# split train, test for calibration\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n    train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\nclf = GaussianNB()\nprob_pos_clf = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with isotonic calibration\nclf_isotonic = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='isotonic')\nprob_pos_isotonic = clf_isotonic.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with sigmoid calibration\nclf_sigmoid = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='sigmoid')\nprob_pos_sigmoid = clf_sigmoid.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\nclf_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_clf, sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_isotonic_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_isotonic,\n                                      sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_sigmoid_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_sigmoid,\n                                     sample_weight=sw_test)\ny_unique = np.unique(y)\ncolors = cm.rainbow(np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, y_unique.size))\norder = np.lexsort((prob_pos_clf, ))\n\n```\nHint: Search `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py` to get the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"CalibrationDisplay\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_digits`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n783 | def load_digits(*, n_class=10, return_X_y=False, as_frame=False):\n784 |     \"\"\"Load and return the digits dataset (classification).\n785 | \n786 |     Each datapoint is a 8x8 image of a digit.\n787 | \n788 |     =================   ==============\n789 |     Classes                         10\n790 |     Samples per class             ~180\n791 |     Samples total                 1797\n792 |     Dimensionality                  64\n793 |     Features             integers 0-16\n794 |     =================   ==============\n795 | \n796 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <digits_dataset>`.\n797 | \n798 |     Parameters\n799 |     ----------\n800 |     n_class : int, default=10\n801 |         The number of classes to return. Between 0 and 10.\n802 | \n803 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n804 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object.\n805 |         See below for more information about the `data` and `target` object.\n806 | \n807 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n808 | \n809 |     as_frame : bool, default=False\n810 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n811 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric). The target is\n812 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target columns.\n813 |         If `return_X_y` is True, then (`data`, `target`) will be pandas\n814 |         DataFrames or Series as described below.\n815 | \n816 |         .. versionadded:: 0.23\n817 | \n818 |     Returns\n819 |     -------\n820 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n821 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n822 | \n823 |         data : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (1797, 64)\n824 |             The flattened data matrix. If `as_frame=True`, `data` will be\n825 |             a pandas DataFrame.\n826 |         target: {ndarray, Series} of shape (1797,)\n827 |             The classification target. If `as_frame=True`, `target` will be\n828 |             a pandas Series.\n829 |         feature_names: list\n830 |             The names of the dataset columns.\n831 |         target_names: list\n832 |             The names of target classes.\n833 | \n834 |             .. versionadded:: 0.20\n835 | \n836 |         frame: DataFrame of shape (1797, 65)\n837 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with `data` and\n838 |             `target`.\n839 | \n840 |             .. versionadded:: 0.23\n841 |         images: {ndarray} of shape (1797, 8, 8)\n842 |             The raw image data.\n843 |         DESCR: str\n844 |             The full description of the dataset.\n845 | \n846 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n847 | \n848 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n849 | \n850 |     This