the same as the\n3303 |         input dtype.\n3304 |     out : ndarray, optional\n3305 |         Alternate output array in which to place the result.  The default\n3306 |         is ``None``; if provided, it must have the same shape as the\n3307 |         expected output, but the type will be cast if necessary.\n3308 |         See :ref:`ufuncs-output-type` for more details.\n3309 | \n3310 |     keepdims : bool, optional\n3311 |         If this is set to True, the axes which are reduced are left\n3312 |         in the result as dimensions with size one. With this option,\n3313 |         the result will broadcast correctly against the input array.\n3314 | \n3315 |         If the default value is passed, then `keepdims` will not be\n3316 |         passed through to the `mean` method of sub-classes of\n3317 |         `ndarray`, however any non-default value will be.  If the\n3318 |         sub-class' method does not implement `keepdims` any\n3319 |         exceptions will be raised.\n3320 | \n3321 |     Returns\n3322 |     -------\n3323 |     m : ndarray, see dtype parameter above\n3324 |         If `out=None`, returns a new array containing the mean values,\n3325 |         otherwise a reference to the output array is returned.\n3326 | \n3327 |     See Also\n3328 |     --------\n3329 |     average : Weighted average\n3330 |     std, var, nanmean, nanstd, nanvar\n3331 | \n3332 |     Notes\n3333 |     -----\n3334 |     The arithmetic mean is the sum of the elements along the axis divided\n3335 |     by the number of elements.\n3336 | \n3337 |     Note that for floating-point input, the mean is computed using the\n3338 |     same precision the input has.  Depending on the input data, this can\n3339 |     cause the results to be inaccurate, especially for `float32` (see\n3340 |     example below).  Specifying a higher-precision accumulator using the\n3341 |     `dtype` keyword can alleviate this issue.\n3342 | \n3343 |     By default, `float16` results are computed using `float32` intermediates\n3344 |     for extra precision.\n3345 | \n3346 |     Examples\n3347 |     --------\n3348 |     >>> a = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\n3349 |     >>> np.mean(a)\n3350 |     2.5\n3351 |     >>> np.mean(a, axis=0)\n3352 |     array([2., 3.])\n3353 |     >>> np.mean(a, axis=1)\n3354 |     array([1.5, 3.5])\n3355 | \n3356 |     In single precision, `mean` can be inaccurate:\n3357 | \n3358 |     >>> a = np.zeros((2, 512*512), dtype=np.float32)\n3359 |     >>> a[0, :] = 1.0\n3360 |     >>> a[1, :] = 0.1\n3361 |     >>> np.mean(a)\n3362 |     0.54999924\n3363 | \n3364 |     Computing the mean in float64 is more accurate:\n3365 | \n3366 |     >>> np.mean(a, dtype=np.float64)\n3367 |     0.55000000074505806 # may vary\n3368 | \n3369 |     \"\"\"\n3370 |     kwargs = {}\n3371 |     if keepdims is not np._NoValue:\n3372 |         kwargs['keepdims'] = keepdims\n3373 |     if type(a) is not mu.ndarray:\n3374 |         try:\n3375 |             mean = a.mean\n3376 |         except AttributeError:\n3377 |             pass\n3378 |         else:\n3379 |             return mean(axis=axis, dtype=dtype, out=out, **kwargs)\n3380 | \n3381 |     return _methods._mean(a, axis=axis, dtype=dtype,\n3382 |                           out=out, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.window`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n109 | def window(self):\n110 |         pass\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/core/fromnumeric.py:_mean_dispatcher`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3274 | def _mean_dispatcher(a, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None, keepdims=None):\n3275 |     return (a, out)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"window_mean\", \"moving_average\", \"window_mean moving_average\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_restrict`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n31 | def check_restrict(cmd):\n32 |     \"\"\"Return the res