 Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the _load_arff_response function since it seems crucial for implementing MD5 checksum validation:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement MD5 checksum validation for downloaded ARFF files. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 800\nline: 801\nline: 814\nline: 815\nfunction: fetch_openml\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491\nline: 492\nline: 493\nline: 494\nline: 495\nline: 496\nline: 497\nline: 498\nline: 499\nline: 500\nline: 501\nline: 502\nline: 503\nline: 504\nline: 505\nline: 506\nline: 507\nline: 508\nline: 509\nline: 510\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 15\nimport: hashlib\n\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py\nline: 1144\nline: 1145\nline: 1146\nline: 1147\nline: 1148\nline: 1149\nline: 1150\nline: 1151\nline: 1152\nline: 1153\nline: 1154\nfunction: _sha256\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 840\nline: 841\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n```\n\nThe main modifications needed are:\n\n1. In `fetch_openml` function:\n   - Need to add checksum validation logic before processing the data\n   - Should use hashlib.md5() to calculate checksum\n   - Need to access the data_description's md5_checksum field\n\n2. In `_load_arff_response` function:\n   - Add checksum verification after loading the data\n   - Compare calculated checksum with data_description's checksum\n\n3. `_base.py` already imports hashlib, so no changes needed there\n\n4. In `_download_data_to_bunch`:\n   - Already handles sparse vs dense data processing\n   - No modifications needed for checksum handling\n\nThese locations form the core areas where the MD5 checksum validation functionality needs to be implemented. The validation should be optional (controlled by a parameter) and performed after downloading but before processing the actual data.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 108267, "completion_tokens": 784}, "time": 104.25475406646729}]}}
{"instance_id": "sympy__sympy-27325", "found_files": [["sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py", "sympy/core/basic.py", "sympy/core/evalf.py"]], "found_modules": [["sympy/matrices/sparse.py:SparseRepMatrix", "sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign", "sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.OutputsContext", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement", "sympy/polys/rootisolation.py:ComplexInterval", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensExpr", "sympy/core/basic.py:Basic", "sympy/stats/crv_types.py:BetaDistribution", "sympy/matrices/expressions/kronecker.py:combine_kronecker", "sympy/physics/secondquant.py:InnerProduct", "sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP_Python", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensorSymmetry", "sympy/functions/combinatorial/numbers.py:genocchi", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Union", "sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.InertiaDeclContext", "sympy/physics/quantum/spin.py:CoupledSpinState", "sympy/functions/elementary/trigonometric.py:cot", "sympy/physics/vector/frame.py:ReferenceFrame", "sympy/series/formal.py:FormalPowerSeries", "sympy/solvers/ode/ode.py:check_nonlinear_2eq_order2", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Intersection", "sympy/utilities/codegen.py:JuliaCodeGen", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries", "sympy/diffgeom/diffgeom.py:LieDerivative", "sympy/sets/fancysets.py:Complexes", "sympy/parsing/latex/_antlr/latexparser.py:LaTeXParser.Exp_nofuncContext", "sympy/geometry/polygon.py:RegularPolygon", "sympy/functions/elementary/hyperbolic.py:acosh", "sympy/core/evalf.py:evalf"]], "found_entities": [["sympy/matrices/sparse.py:SparseRepMatrix.row_structure_symbolic_cholesky", "sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign._eval_rewrite_as_Abs", "sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.OutputsContext.Output", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement.max_norm", "sympy/polys/rootisolation.py:ComplexInterval.center", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement.l1_norm", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet.__len__", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensExpr.__rpow__", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet.as_relational", "sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensExpr._replace_indices", "sympy/core/basic.py:Basic._rewrite", "sympy/stats/crv_types.py:BetaDistribution._characteristic_function", "sympy/matrices/expressions/kronecker.py:combine_kronecker.haskron", "sympy/physics/secondquant.py:InnerProduct.ket", "sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP_Python._nth", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensorSymmetry.base", "sympy/physics/secondquant.py:InnerProduct.__repr__", "sympy/functions/combinatorial/numbers.py:genocchi._eval_evalf", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Union.__iter__", "sympy/physics/secondquant.py:InnerProduct.__str__", "sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP_Python.l1_norm", "sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.InertiaDeclContext.getRuleIndex", "sympy/physics/quantum/spin.py:CoupledSpinState.jn", "sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP_Python.l2_norm_squared", "sympy/functions/elementary/trigonometric.py:cot._eval_rewrite_as_tan", "sympy/physics/quantum/spin.py:CoupledSpinState.coupling", "sympy/physics/vector/frame.py:ReferenceFrame.y", "sympy/series/formal.py:FormalPowerSeries.__rsub__", "sympy/physics/quantum/spin.py:CoupledSpinState.coupled_jn", "sympy/functions/elementary/trigonometric.py:cot._eval_rewrite_as_csc", "sympy/solvers/ode/ode.py:check_nonlinear_2eq_order2", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Intersection.identity", "sympy/utilities/codegen.py:JuliaCodeGen.dump_jl", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Intersection.zero", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries.gfps", "sympy/physics/vector/frame.py:ReferenceFrame.yz", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries.f", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries.g", "sympy/diffgeom/diffgeom.py:LieDerivative.v_field", "sympy/physics/vector/frame.py:ReferenceFrame.zy", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Intersection.args", "sympy/sets/fancysets.py:Complexes.sets", "sympy/parsing/latex/_antlr/latexparser.py:LaTeXParser.Exp_nofuncContext.expr", "sympy/sets/sets.py:Intersection.is_iterable", "sympy/geometry/polygon.py:RegularPolygon.__str__", "sympy/physics/vector/frame.py:ReferenceFrame.u", "sympy/parsing/latex/_antlr/latexparser.py:LaTeXParser.Exp_nofuncContext.subexpr", "sympy/parsing/latex/_antlr/latexparser.py:LaTeXParser.Exp_nofuncContext.getRuleIndex", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries.truncate", "sympy/functions/elementary/hyperbolic.py:acosh._eval_rewrite_as_log", "sympy/series/formal.py:FiniteFormalPowerSeries._eval_derivative", "sympy/core/evalf.py:evalf"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the analysis of the codebase, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the required format:\n\n```\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nline: 439\nfunction: sign._eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/core/basic.py\nline: 2078-2089\nfunction: Basic.rewrite\n\nsympy/core/basic.py\nline: 2108-2110\nfunction: Basic._rewrite\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nline: 421-422\nfunction: sign._eval_rewrite\n\nsympy/core/evalf.py\nline: 1452-1537\nfunction: evalf\n```\n\nThis feature request requires modifying how the `rewrite` method handles the conversion between `sign` and `Abs` (absolute value) expressions. The main issue is that when calling `rewrite(sign(x), abs)`, it doesn't properly dispatch to the `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` method because it's not special-cased for the built-in `abs` function.\n\nThe key areas identified above show where the core rewriting functionality exists and where the `abs` conversion happens. The main modifications would need to be made in the `sign` class's `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` method and how the rewrite system handles the conversion between different mathematical functions."], "meta_data": {"repo": "sympy/sympy", "base_commit": "fbe647fb34f90de01946fcaa1314207f11e5b26e", "problem_statement": "rewrite(abs) should call rewrite(Abs)\n```py\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(abs)\nsign(x)\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(Abs)\nPiecewise((0, Eq(x, 0)), (x/Abs(x), True))\n```\n\nThis is because `rewrite(x)` just dispatches to the method called `_eval_rewrite_as_x`, and the method here is called `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` since `Abs` is the name of the class. But most people use `abs`, the built-in function which dispatches to `Abs`. So it should be special-cased in rewrite to call `Abs`. \n", "patch": "diff --git a/sympy/core/basic.py b/sympy/core/basic.py\nindex 62ccd3ed5508..f9f04c728245 100644\n--- a/sympy/core/basic.py\n+++ b/sympy/core/basic.py\n@@ -2075,6 +2075,11 @@ def rewrite(self, *args, deep=True, **hints):\n         pattern = args[:-1]\n         rule = args[-1]\n \n+        # Special case: map `abs` to `Abs`\n+        if rule is abs:\n+            from sympy.functions.elementary.complexes import Abs\n+            rule = Abs\n+\n         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n         if isinstance(rule, str):\n             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 65658, "completion_tokens": 526}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'sympy' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: rewrite(abs) should call rewrite(Abs)\n\n```py\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(abs)\nsign(x)\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(Abs)\nPiecewise((0, Eq(x, 0)), (x/Abs(x), True))\n```\n\nThis is because `rewrite(x)` just dispatches to the method called `_eval_rewrite_as_x`, and the method here is called `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` since `Abs` is the name of the class. But most people use `abs`, the built-in function which dispatches to `Abs`. So it should be special-cased in rewrite to call `Abs`.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style analysis):\n# Unique Artifacts\n\nUse these unique artifacts as supplemental hints, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace A role: `feature_positive`\n- Trace B role: `feature_negative`\n- Comparison: `trace_a_minus_trace_b`\n- Unique functions in JSON: `120`\n- Unique functions shown here: `107`\n- Target package filter: `sympy`\n\n## Related Files\n- `sympy/core/expr.py`: `20` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/numbers.py`: `20` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/function.py`: `10` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/basic.py`: `7` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/printing/str.py`: `7` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/add.py`: `6` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/evalf.py`: `5` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/mul.py`: `5` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py`: `5` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/traversal.py`: `4` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/core/sympify.py`: `3` unique function(s)\n- `sympy/printing/precedence.py`: `3` unique function(s)\n\n## Artifact Candidates\n- artifact: `sympy/core/traversal.py:preorder_traversal._preorder_traversal`\n  - module: `sympy.core.traversal`\n  - definition: `lines 117-138`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 118`\n  - observed lines: `118, 119, 122, 123, 128, 129, 134, 135`\n  - trace activity: 1119 events, 239 calls, 641 line events, 239 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 21\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 118`: `yield node`\n    - `line 119`: `if self._skip_flag:`\n    - `line 122`: `if isinstance(node, Basic):`\n- artifact: `sympy/core/numbers.py:mpf_norm`\n  - module: `sympy.core.numbers`\n  - definition: `lines 150-177`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 162`\n  - observed lines: `162, 163, 175, 176, 177`\n  - trace activity: 546 events, 78 calls, 390 line events, 78 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 30\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 162`: `sign, man, expt, bc = mpf`\n    - `line 163`: `if not man:`\n    - `line 175`: `from mpmath.libmp.backend import MPZ`\n- artifact: `sympy/core/expr.py:Expr.as_terms`\n  - module: `sympy.core.expr`\n  - definition: `lines 1177-1229`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1179`\n  - observed lines: `1179, 1181, 1183, 1184, 1186, 1187, 1189, 1190, ...`\n  - trace activity: 479 events, 8 calls, 463 line events, 8 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 22\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1179`: `from .exprtools import decompose_power`\n    - `line 1181`: `gens, terms = set(), []`\n    - `line 1183`: `for term in Add.make_args(self):`\n- artifact: `sympy/printing/precedence.py:precedence`\n  - module: `sympy.printing.precedence`\n  - definition: `lines 118-132`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 123`\n  - observed lines: `123, 125, 126, 127, 128, 129, 130, 131, ...`\n  - trace activity: 476 events, 27 calls, 422 line events, 27 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 24\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 123`: `if hasattr(item, \"precedence\"):`\n    - `line 125`: `if not isinstance(item, type):`\n    - `line 126`: `for i in type(item).mro():`\n- artifact: `sympy/core/evalf.py:EvalfMixin.evalf`\n  - module: `sympy.core.evalf`\n  - definition: `lines 1571-1692`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1636`\n  - observed lines: `1636, 1637, 1639, 1643, 1650, 1652, 1653, 1654, ...`\n  - trace activity: 437 events, 19 calls, 399 line events, 19 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 24\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1636`: `from .numbers import