\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | =================================\n 3 | Ways to set a color's alpha value\n 4 | =================================\n 5 | \n 6 | Compare setting alpha by the *alpha* keyword argument and by one of the Matplotlib color\n 7 | formats. Often, the *alpha* keyword is the only tool needed to add transparency to a\n 8 | color. In some cases, the *(matplotlib_color, alpha)* color format provides an easy way\n 9 | to fine-tune the appearance of a Figure.\n10 | \n11 | \"\"\"\n12 | \n13 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n14 | import numpy as np\n15 | \n16 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility.\n17 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n18 | \n19 | fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(ncols=2, figsize=(8, 4))\n20 | \n21 | x_values = [n for n in range(20)]\n22 | y_values = np.random.randn(20)\n23 | \n24 | facecolors = ['green' if y > 0 else 'red' for y in y_values]\n25 | edgecolors = facecolors\n26 | \n27 | ax1.bar(x_values, y_values, color=facecolors, edgecolor=edgecolors, alpha=0.5)\n28 | ax1.set_title(\"Explicit 'alpha' keyword value\\nshared by all bars and edges\")\n29 | \n30 | \n31 | # Normalize y values to get distinct face alpha values.\n32 | abs_y = [abs(y) for y in y_values]\n33 | face_alphas = [n / max(abs_y) for n in abs_y]\n34 | edge_alphas = [1 - alpha for alpha in face_alphas]\n35 | \n36 | colors_with_alphas = list(zip(facecolors, face_alphas))\n37 | edgecolors_with_alphas = list(zip(edgecolors, edge_alphas))\n38 | \n39 | ax2.bar(x_values, y_values, color=colors_with_alphas,\n40 |         edgecolor=edgecolors_with_alphas)\n41 | ax2.set_title('Normalized alphas for\\neach bar and each edge')\n42 | \n43 | plt.show()\n44 | \n45 | # %%\n46 | #\n47 | # .. admonition:: References\n48 | #\n49 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n50 | #    in this example:\n51 | #\n52 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar`\n53 | #    - `matplotlib.pyplot.subplots`\n54 | #\n55 | # .. tags::\n56 | #\n57 | #    styling: color\n58 | #    plot-type: bar\n59 | #    level: beginner\n60 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/broken_barh.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ===========\n 3 | Broken Barh\n 4 | ===========\n 5 | \n 6 | Make a \"broken\" horizontal bar plot, i.e., one with gaps\n 7 | \"\"\"\n 8 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 9 | \n10 | # Horizontal bar plot with gaps\n11 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n12 | ax.broken_barh([(110, 30), (150, 10)], (10, 9), facecolors='tab:blue')\n13 | ax.broken_barh([(10, 50), (100, 20), (130, 10)], (20, 9),\n14 |                facecolors=('tab:orange', 'tab:green', 'tab:red'))\n15 | ax.set_ylim(5, 35)\n16 | ax.set_xlim(0, 200)\n17 | ax.set_xlabel('seconds since start')\n18 | ax.set_yticks([15, 25], labels=['Bill', 'Jim'])     # Modify y-axis tick labels\n19 | ax.grid(True)                                       # Make grid lines visible\n20 | ax.annotate('race interrupted', (61, 25),\n21 |             xytext=(0.8, 0.9), textcoords='axes fraction',\n22 |             arrowprops=dict(facecolor='black', shrink=0.05),\n23 |             fontsize=16,\n24 |             horizontalalignment='right', verticalalignment='top')\n25 | \n26 | plt.show()\n27 | \n28 | # %%\n29 | # .. tags::\n30 | #\n31 | #    component: annotation\n32 | #    plot-type: bar\n33 | #    level: beginner\n34 | \n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==========\n 3 | Hatch demo\n 4 | ==========\n 5 | \n 6 | Hatches can be added to most polygons in Matplotlib, including `~.Axes.bar`,\n 7 | `~.Axes.fill_between`, `~.Axes.contourf`, and children of `~.patches.Polygon`.\n 8 | They are currently supported in the PS, PDF, SVG, macosx, and Agg backends. The WX\n 9 | and Cairo backends do not currently support hatching.\n10 | \n11 | See also :doc:`/gallery/images_contours_and_fields/contourf_hatching` for\n12 | an example using `~.Axes.contourf`, and\n13 | :doc:`/gallery/shapes_and_collections/hatch_style_reference` for swatches\n14 | of the existing hatches.\n15 | \n16 | \"\"\"\n17 | \n18 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n19 | import numpy as np\n20 | \n21 | from matplotlib.patches import Ellipse, Polygon\n22 | \n23 | x = np.arange(1, 5)\n24 | y1 = np.arange(1, 5)\n25 | y2 = np.ones(y1.shape) * 4\n26 | \n27 | fig = plt.figure()\n28 | axs = fig.subplot_mosaic([['bar1', 'patches'], ['bar2', 'patches']])\n29 | \n30 | axs['bar1'].bar(x, y1, edgecolor='black', hatch=\"/\")\n31 | axs['bar1'].bar(x, y2, bottom=y1, edgecolor='black', hatch='//')\n32 | \n33 | axs['bar2'].bar(x, y1, edgecolor='black', hatch=['--', '+', 'x', '\\\\'])\n34 | axs['bar2'].bar(x, y2, bottom=y1, edgecolor='black',\n35 |                 hatch=['*', 'o', 'O', '.'])\n36 | \n37 | x = np.arange(0, 40, 0.2)\n38 | axs['patches'].fill_between(x, np.sin(x) * 4 + 30, y2=0,\n39 |                             hatch='///', zorder=2, fc='c')\n40 | axs['patches'].add_patch(Ellipse((4, 50), 10, 10, fill=True,\n41 |                                  hatch='*', facecolor='y'))\n42 | axs['patches'].add_patch(Polygon([(10, 20), (30, 50), (50, 10)],\n43 |                                  hatch='\\\\/...', facecolor='g'))\n44 | axs['patches'].set_xlim([0, 40])\n45 | axs['patches'].set_ylim([10, 60])\n46 | axs['patches'].set_aspect(1)\n47 | plt.show()\n48 | \n49 | # %%\n50 | #\n51 | # .. admonition:: References\n52 | #\n53 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n54 | #    in this example:\n55 | #\n56 | #    - `matplotlib.patches`\n57 | #    - `matplotlib.patches.Ellipse`\n58 | #    - `matplotlib.patches.Polygon`\n59 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.add_patch`\n60 | #    - `matplotlib.patches.Patch.set_hatch`\n61 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar` / `matplotlib.pyplot.bar`\n62 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/statistics/histogram_multihist.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =====================================================\n  3 | The histogram (hist) function with multiple data sets\n  4 | =====================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | Plot histogram with multiple sample sets and demonstrate:\n  7 | \n  8 | * Use of legend with multiple sample sets\n  9 | * Stacked bars\n 10 | * Step curve with no fill\n 11 | * Data sets of different sample sizes\n 12 | \n 13 | Selecting different bin counts and sizes can significantly affect the\n 14 | shape of a histogram. The Astropy docs have a great section on how to\n 15 | select these parameters:\n 16 | http://docs.astropy.org/en/stable/visualization/histogram.html\n 17 | \n 18 | .. redirect-from:: /gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/filled_step\n 19 | \n 20 | \"\"\"\n 21 | # %%\n 22 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 23 | import numpy as np\n 24 | \n 25 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n 26 | \n 27 | n_bins = 10\n 28 | x = np.random.randn(1000, 3)\n 29 | \n 30 | fig, ((ax0, ax1), (ax2, ax3)) = plt.subplots(nrows=2, ncols=2)\n 31 | \n 32 | colors = ['red', 'tan', 'lime']\n 33 | ax0.hist(x, n_bins, density=True, histtype='bar', color=colors, label=colors)\n 34 | ax0.legend(prop={'size': 10})\n 35 | ax0.set_title('bars with legend')\n 36 | \n 37 | ax1.hist(x, n_bins, density=True, histtype='bar', stacked=True)\n 38 | ax1.set_title('stacked bar')\n 39 | \n 40 | ax2.hist(x, n_bins, histtype='step', stacked=True, fill=False)\n 41 | ax2.set_title('stack step (unfilled)')\n 42 | \n 43 | # Make a multiple-histogram of data-sets with different length.\n 44 | x_multi = [np.random.randn(n) for n in [10000, 5000, 2000]]\n 45 | ax3.hist(x_multi, n_bins, histtype='bar')\n 46 | ax3.set_title('different sample sizes')\n 47 | \n 48 | fig.tight_layout()\n 49 | plt.show()\n 50 | \n 51 | # %%\n 52 | # -----------------------------------\n 53 | # Setting properties for each dataset\n 54 | # -----------------------------------\n 55 | #\n 56 | # You can style the histograms individually by passing a list of values to the\n 57 | # following parameters:\n 58 | #\n 59 | # * edgecolor\n 60 | # * facecolor\n 61 | # * hatch\n 62 | # * linewidth\n 63 | # * linestyle\n 64 | #\n 65 | #\n 66 | # edgecolor\n 67 | # .........\n 68 | \n 69 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 70 | \n 71 | edgecolors = ['green', 'red', 'blue']\n 72 | \n 73 | ax.hist(x, n_bins, fill=False, histtype=\"step\", stacked=True,\n 74 |         edgecolor=edgecolors, label=edgecolors)\n 75 | ax.legend()\n 76 | ax.set_title('Stacked Steps with Edgecolors')\n 77 | \n 78 | plt.show()\n 79 | \n 80 | # %%\n 81 | # facecolor\n 82 | # .........\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"bar facecolor\", \"bar edgecolor\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `lib/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/axes3d.py:Axes3D.bar`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2989 | def bar(self, left, height, zs=0, zdir='z', *args,\n2990 |             axlim_clip=False, **kwargs):\n2991 |         \"\"\"\n2992 |         Add 2D bar(s).\n2993 | \n2994 |         Parameters\n2995 |         ----------\n2996 |         left : 1D array-like\n2997 |             The x coordinates of the left sides of the bars.\n2998 |         height : 1D array-like\n2999 |             The height of the bars.\n3000 |         zs : float or 1D array-like, default: 0\n3001 |             Z coordinate of bars; if a single value is specified, it will be\n3002 |             used for all bars.\n3003 |         zdir : {'x', 'y', 'z'}, default: 'z'\n3004 |             When plotting 2D data, the direction to use as z ('x', 'y' or 'z').\n3005 |         axlim_clip : bool, default: False\n3006 |             Whether to hide bars with points outside the axes view limits.\n3007 | \n3008 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n3009 |         data : indexable object, optional\n3010 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n3011 |         **kwargs\n3012 |             Other keyword arguments are forwarded to\n3013 |             `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar`.\n3014 | \n3015 |         Returns\n3016 |         -------\n3017 |         mpl_toolkits.mplot3d.art3d.Patch3DCollection\n3018 |         \"\"\"\n3019 |         had_data = self.has_data()\n3020 | \n3021 |         patches = super().bar(left, height, *args, **kwargs)\n3022 | \n3023 |         zs = np.broadcast_to(zs, len(left), subok=True)\n3024 | \n3025 |         verts = []\n3026 |         verts_zs = []\n3027 |         for p, z in zip(patches, zs):\n3028 |             vs = art3d._get_patch_verts(p)\n3029 |             verts += vs.tolist()\n3030 |             verts_zs += [z] * len(vs)\n3031 |             art3d.patch_2d_to_3d(p, z, zdir, axlim_clip)\n3032 |             if 'alpha' in kwargs:\n3033 |                 p.set_alpha(kwargs['alpha'])\n3034 | \n3035 |         if len(verts) > 0:\n3036 |             # the following has to be skipped if verts is empty\n3037 |             # NOTE: Bugs could still occur if len(verts) > 0,\n3038 |             #       but the \"2nd dimension\" is empty.\n3039 |             xs, ys = zip(*verts)\n3040 |         else:\n3041 |             xs, ys = [], []\n3042 | \n3043 |         xs, ys, verts_zs = art3d.juggle_axes(xs, ys, verts_zs, zdir)\n3044 |         self.auto_scale_xyz(xs, ys, verts_zs, had_data)\n3045 | \n3046 |         return patches\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"bar facecolor\", \"bar() color bar facecolor bar edgecolor\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `galleries/examples/misc/logos2.py:create_icon_axes`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n37 | def create_icon_axes(fig, ax_position, lw_bars, lw_grid, lw_border, rgrid):\n38 |     \"\"\"\n39 |     Create a polar Axes containing the matplotlib radar plot.\n40 | \n41 |     Parameters\n42 |     ----------\n43 |     fig : matplotlib.figure.Figure\n44 |         The figure to draw into.\n45 |     ax_position : (float, float, float, float)\n46 |         The position of the created Axes in figure coordinates as\n47 |         (x, y, width, height).\n48 |     lw_bars : float\n49 |         The linewidth of the bars.\n50 |     lw_grid : float\n51 |         The linewidth of the grid.\n52 |     lw_border : float\n53 |         The linewidth of the Axes border.\n54 |     rgrid : array-like\n55 |         Positions of the radial grid.\n56 | \n57 |     Returns\n58 |     -------\n59 |     ax : matplotlib.axes.Axes\n60 |         The created Axes.\n61 |     \"\"\"\n62 |     with plt.rc_context({'axes.edgecolor': MPL_BLUE,\n63 |                          'axes.linewidth': lw_border}):\n64 |         ax = fig.add_axes(ax_position, projection='polar')\n65 |         ax.set_axisbelow(True)\n66 | \n67 |         N = 7\n68 |         arc = 2. * np.pi\n69 |         theta = np.arange(0.0, arc, arc / N)\n70 |         radii = np.array([2, 6, 8, 7, 4, 5, 8])\n71 |         width = np.pi / 4 * np.array([0.4, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 0.2, 0.5, 0.3])\n72 |         bars = ax.bar(theta, radii, width=width, bottom=0.0, align='edge',\n73 |                       edgecolor='0.3', lw=lw_bars)\n74 |         for r, bar in zip(radii, bars):\n75 |             color = *cm.jet(r / 10.)[:3], 0.6  # color from jet with alpha=0.6\n76 |             bar.set_facecolor(color)\n77 | \n78 |         ax.tick_params(labelbottom=False, labeltop=False,\n79 |                        labelleft=False, labelright=False)\n80 | \n81 |         ax.grid(lw=lw_grid, color='0.9')\n82 |         ax.set_rmax(9)\n83 |         ax.set_yticks(rgrid)\n84 | \n85 |         # the actual visible background - extends a bit beyond the axis\n86 |         ax.add_patch(Rectangle((0, 0), arc, 9.58,\n87 |                                facecolor='white', zorder=0,\n88 |                                clip_on=False, in_layout=False))\n89 |         return ax\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"bar edgecolor\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/color/set_alpha.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | =================================\n 