n not be None for lxml and StringIO input\n 844 |     encoding: str | None = \"utf-8\",\n 845 |     parser: XMLParsers = \"lxml\",\n 846 |     stylesheet: FilePath | ReadBuffer[bytes] | ReadBuffer[str] | None = None,\n 847 |     iterparse: dict[str, list[str]] | None = None,\n 848 |     compression: CompressionOptions = \"infer\",\n 849 |     storage_options: StorageOptions | None = None,\n 850 |     dtype_backend: DtypeBackend | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n 851 | ) -> DataFrame:\n 852 |     r\"\"\"\n 853 |     Read XML document into a :class:`~pandas.DataFrame` object.\n 854 | \n 855 |     .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n 856 | \n 857 |     Parameters\n 858 |     ----------\n 859 |     path_or_buffer : str, path object, or file-like object\n 860 |         String, path object (implementing ``os.PathLike[str]``), or file-like\n 861 |         object implementing a ``read()`` function. The string can be a path.\n 862 |         The string can further be a URL. Valid URL schemes\n 863 |         include http, ftp, s3, and file.\n 864 | \n 865 |         .. deprecated:: 2.1.0\n 866 |             Passing xml literal strings is deprecated.\n 867 |             Wrap literal xml input in ``io.StringIO`` or ``io.BytesIO`` instead.\n 868 | \n 869 |     xpath : str, optional, default './\\*'\n 870 |         The ``XPath`` to parse required set of nodes for migration to\n 871 |         :class:`~pandas.DataFrame`.``XPath`` should return a collection of elements\n 872 |         and not a single element. Note: The ``etree`` parser supports limited ``XPath``\n 873 |         expressions. For more complex ``XPath``, use ``lxml`` which requires\n 874 |         installation.\n 875 | \n 876 |     namespaces : dict, optional\n 877 |         The namespaces defined in XML document as dicts with key being\n 878 |         namespace prefix and value the URI. There is no need to include all\n 879 |         namespaces in XML, only the ones used in ``xpath`` expression.\n 880 |         Note: if XML document uses default namespace denoted as\n 881 |         `xmlns='<URI>'` without a prefix, you must assign any temporary\n 882 |         namespace prefix such as 'doc' to the URI in order to parse\n 883 |         underlying nodes and/or attributes.\n 884 | \n 885 |     elems_only : bool, optional, default False\n 886 |         Parse only the child elements at the specified ``xpath``. By default,\n 887 |         all child elements and non-empty text nodes are returned.\n 888 | \n 889 |     attrs_only :  bool, optional, default False\n 890 |         Parse only the attributes at the specified ``xpath``.\n 891 |         By default, all attributes are returned.\n 892 | \n 893 |     names :  list-like, optional\n 894 |         Column names for DataFrame of parsed XML data. Use this parameter to\n 895 |         rename original element names and distinguish same named elements and\n 896 |         attributes.\n 897 | \n 898 |     dtype : Type name or dict of column -> type, optional\n 899 |         Data type for data or columns. E.g. {{'a': np.float64, 'b': np.int32,\n 900 |         'c': 'Int64'}}\n 901 |         Use `str` or `object` together with suitable `na_values` settings\n 902 |         to preserve and not interpret dtype.\n 903 |         If converters are specified, they will be applied INSTEAD\n 904 |         of dtype conversion.\n 905 | \n 906 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n 907 | \n 908 |     converters : dict, optional\n 909 |         Dict of functions for converting values in certain columns. Keys can either\n 910 |         be integers or column labels.\n 911 | \n 912 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n 913 | \n 914 |     parse_dates : bool or list of int or names or list of lists or dict, default False\n 915 |         Identifiers to parse index or columns to datetime. The behavior is as follows:\n 916 | \n 917 |         * boolean. If True -> try parsing the index.\n 918 |         * list of int or names. e.g. If [1, 2, 3] -> try parsing columns 1, 2, 3\n 919 |           each as a separate date column.\n 920 |         * list of lists. e.g.  If [[1, 3]] -> combine columns 1 and 3 and parse as\n 921 |           a single date column.\n 922 |         * dict, e.g. {{'foo' : [1, 3]}} -> parse columns 1, 3 as date and call\n 923 |           result 'foo'\n 924 | \n 925 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n 926 | \n 927 |     encoding : str, optional, default 'utf-8'\n 928 |         Encoding of XML document.\n 929 | \n 930 |     parser : {{'lxml','etree'}}, default 'lxml'\n 931 |         Parser module to use for retrieval of data. Only 'lxml' and\n 932 |         'etree' are supported. With 'lxml' more complex ``XPath`` searches\n 933 |         and ability to use XSLT stylesheet are supported.\n 934 | \n 935 |     stylesheet : str, path object or file-like object\n 936 |         A URL, file-like object, or a string path containing an XSLT script.\n 937 |         This stylesheet should flatten complex, deeply nested XML documents\n 938 |         for easier parsing. To use this feature you must have ``lxml`` module\n 939 |         installed and specify 'lxml' as ``parser``. The ``xpath`` must\n 940 |         reference nodes of transformed XML document generated after XSLT\n 941 |         transformation and not the original XML document. Only XSLT 1.0\n 942 |         scripts and not later versions is currently supported.\n 943 | \n 944 |     iterparse : dict, optional\n 945 |         The nodes or attributes to retrieve in iterparsing of XML document\n 946 |         as a dict with key being the name of repeating element and value being\n 947 |         list of elements or attribute names that are descendants of the repeated\n 948 |         element. Note: If this option is used, it will replace ``xpath`` parsing\n 949 |         and unlike ``xpath``, descendants do not need to relate to each other but can\n 950 |         exist any where in document under the repeating element. This memory-\n 951 |         efficient method should be used for very large XML files (500MB, 1GB, or 5GB+).\n 952 |         For example, ``{{\"row_element\": [\"child_elem\", \"attr\", \"grandchild_elem\"]}}``.\n 953 | \n 954 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n 955 | \n 956 |     {decompression_options}\n 957 | \n 958 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0 Zstandard support.\n 959 | \n 960 |     {storage_options}\n 961 | \n 962 |     dtype_backend : {{'numpy_nullable', 'pyarrow'}}\n 963 |         Back-end data type applied to the resultant :class:`DataFrame`\n 964 |         (still experimental). If not specified, the default behavior\n 965 |         is to not use nullable data types. If specified, the behavior\n 966 |         is as follows:\n 967 | \n 968 |         * ``\"numpy_nullable\"``: returns nullable-dtype-backed :class:`DataFrame`\n 969 |         * ``\"pyarrow\"``: returns pyarrow-backed nullable\n 970 |           :class:`ArrowDtype` :class:`DataFrame`\n 971 | \n 972 |         .. versionadded:: 2.0\n 973 | \n 974 |     Returns\n 975 |     -------\n 976 |     df\n 977 |         A DataFrame.\n 978 | \n 979 |     See Also\n 980 |     --------\n 981 |     read_json : Convert a JSON string to pandas object.\n 982 |     read_html : Read HTML tables into a list of DataFrame objects.\n 983 | \n 984 |     Notes\n 985 |     -----\n 986 |     This method is best designed to import shallow XML documents in\n 987 |     following format which is the ideal fit for the two-dimensions of a\n 988 |     ``DataFrame`` (row by column). ::\n 989 | \n 990 |             <root>\n 991 |                 <row>\n 992 |                   <column1>data</column1>\n 993 |                   <column2>data</column2>\n 994 |                   <column3>data</column3>\n 995 |                   ...\n 996 |                </row>\n 997 |                <row>\n 998 |                   ...\n 999 |                </row>\n1000 |                ...\n1001 |             </root>\n1002 | \n1003 |     As a file format, XML documents can be designed any way including\n1004 |     layout of elements and attributes as long as it conforms to W3C\n1005 |     specifications. Therefore, this method is a convenience handler for\n1006 |     a specific flatter design and not all possible XML structures.\n1007 | \n1008 |     However, for more complex XML documents, ``stylesheet`` allows you to\n1009 |     temporarily redesign original document with XSLT (a special purpose\n1010 |     language) for a flatter version for migration to a DataFrame.\n1011 | \n1012 |     This function will *always* return a single :class:`DataFrame` or raise\n1013 |     exceptions due to issues with XML document, ``xpath``, or other\n1014 |     parameters.\n1015 | \n1016 |     See the :ref:`read_xml documentation in the IO section of the docs\n1017 |     <io.read_xml>` for more information in using this method to parse XML\n1018 |     files to DataFrames.\n1019 | \n1020 |     Examples\n1021 |     --------\n1022 |     >>> from io import StringIO\n1023 |     >>> xml = '''<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>\n1024 |     ... <data xmlns=\"http://example.com\">\n1025 |     ...  <row>\n1026 |     ...    <shape>square</shape>\n1027 |     ...    <degrees>360</degrees>\n1028 |     ...    <sides>4.0</sides>\n1029 |     ...  </row>\n1030 |     ...  <row>\n1031 |     ...    <shape>circle</shape>\n1032 |     ...    <degrees>360</degrees>\n1033 |     ...    <sides/>\n1034 |     ...  </row>\n1035 |     ...  <row>\n1036 |     ...    <shape>triangle</shape>\n1037 |     ...    <degrees>180</degrees>\n1038 |     ...    <sides>3.0</sides>\n1039 |     ...  </row>\n1040 |     ... </data>'''\n1041 | \n1042 |     >>> df = pd.read_xml(StringIO(xml))\n1043 |     >>> df\n1044 |           shape  degrees  sides\n1045 |     0    square      360    4.0\n1046 |     1    circle      360    NaN\n1047 |     2  triangle      180    3.0\n1048 | \n1049 |     >>> xml = '''<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>\n1050 |     ... <data>\n1051 |     ...   <row shape=\"square\" degrees=\"360\" sides=\"4.0\"/>\n